<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Asurar Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1639</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-mughal-conflicts">Ahom–Mughal conflicts</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Asurar Ali was a peace agreement concluded in 1639 between the Ahom kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> after more than twenty years of war over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamrup">Kamrup</a>. Negotiated by the Ahom official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/momai-tamuli-borbarua">Momai Tamuli Borbarua</a> and the Mughal commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> Yar Khan, it fixed the boundary at the Barnadi river on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> and the Asurar Ali road on the south bank, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a> to the Mughals.</p></div>
